  "worked_solution": "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -11 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 12 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
